# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings.  Added 2026-09-21.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE WRITING THIS, because a careless `* text=auto` on a repo that
# already holds CRLF blobs rewrites every one of them in a single commit and
# buries real history under a renormalisation diff.  `git ls-files --eol` on
# 1,252 tracked files reported:
#
#     i/lf  w/lf    772      i/lf  w/crlf  461      i/none  11
#     i/lf  w/mixed   6      i/-text w/-text  2     i/crlf   0   <-- the number that matters
#
# ZERO files carry CRLF in the index: core.autocrlf=true has been normalising
# on the way in all along.  So these rules codify what is already true and
# rewrite nothing.  The 461 CRLF files are working-tree only, which is exactly
# what autocrlf=true is meant to produce.
#
# WHY BOTHER, given autocrlf already does it: core.autocrlf is per-machine local
# config.  It is not in the repo, so a fresh clone, a second machine, or an agent
# running under a different git config does not inherit it.  .gitattributes
# travels with the tree and is authoritative over autocrlf.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto

# Windows shells are the one place the ending is FUNCTIONAL rather than
# cosmetic: cmd can mis-parse a multi-line block, a label or a `goto` in a
# .bat checked out with bare LF.  Today the tracked .bat files disagree with
# each other (some w/lf, some w/crlf) because several were written directly by
# tooling rather than checked out by git.  This pins them.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touch these: the bytes are the content.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.db    binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
